# Rust build output
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Local player profile: identity keys, settings, hand history.
#
# The client is portable, so the profile lives next to the binary - which
# means it can appear at any depth, wherever somebody ran one. Unanchored
# for exactly that reason: `/profile/` matched the root only, and a client
# run from a tracked subdirectory would have written an unignored key pair.
profile/
/dist/

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
/vendor/**/target/
/profile/

# The vendored C for D-019's Tox transport IS committed, and the note that used
# to sit here saying otherwise has been withdrawn on its own terms.
#
# It read: "12.7 MB for a transport whose whole point - a measurement across two
# networks - has not been taken yet; the script carries the argument and the
# hashes, and says what would change the answer." The measurement has now been
# taken many times over, and this client carries **two patches** to toxcore:
# `patches/0001` keeps the TCP relay fallback alive, and `patches/0002` asks for
# the missing message when the receive ring is full. A tree that is fetched and
# then patched at build time has a failure mode a committed one does not - a
# library that looks right, links, runs, and is missing the fix - and this
# project has already lost a day to a defect that only showed under measurement.
#
# So the trees are here, patched, at the commits `tools/build-tox.ps1` records:
#   c-toxcore  v0.2.23        d9ca3c577e5abd4303d180eb5270167b4133ea4c
#   cmp                       52bfcfa17d2eb4322da2037ad625f5575129cece
#   libsodium  1.0.20-RELEASE 9511c982fb1d046470a8b42aa36556cdb7da15de
# `patches/` stays as the record of what differs from those commits, which is
# what a reader needs and what a version bump needs.
#
# Their own `.gitignore` files come with them and keep their build output out:
# `obj`, `bin` and `build` are ignored by upstream's rules, not by ours.

